WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.220
IPC testing should include more than successful request and response pairs.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:19.240
Tests should cover malformed messages, truncated messages, oversized messages, unexpected sequence, disconnects, timeouts, repeated operations, slow peers, and cleanup after protocol failures.

3
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:22.940
These cases are ordinary realities for robust IPC code.

4
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:29.780
Permission tests should verify which users, services, or processes can access endpoints.

5
00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:37.080
If an endpoint is meant only for a specific service account or user boundary, that expectation should be tested in the environment where it matters.

6
00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:41.660
Endpoint permissions and deployment configuration are part of the behavior.

7
00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:47.860
Concurrency tests are important when multiple clients, shared state, or shared memory are involved.

8
00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:55.080
Exercise simultaneous clients, writer and reader ordering, cleanup paths, restart recovery, and abandoned resources.

9
00:00:55.180 --> 00:01:01.340
Bugs in IPC often appear when timing changes, not when one client politely sends one request at a time.

10
00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:09.140
Static analysis, compiler warnings, sanitizers, and runtime diagnostics can support IPC code quality.

11
00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:17.960
They can reveal unchecked return values, suspicious length handling, memory safety issues, leaks, races in test builds, and cleanup mistakes.

12
00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:22.780
Tools are most useful when the code structure makes endpoint and message ownership clear.

13
00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:29.780
Logging should provide useful diagnostics without exposing sensitive payloads or internal authorization details.

14
00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:39.720
Code review should trace endpoint creation, permissions, peer identity checks, message parsing, dispatch, state changes, cleanup, and error handling.

15
00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:45.740
A reviewer should be able to explain what each peer may do and how the service behaves when the protocol is not followed.
